From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 09:19:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(model): a model that can park is parked where it would
 be stopped for room

A backend that can park a model answers the Free RPC by moving the model's
weights from the device to host memory and keeping everything else: its
process, its tokenizer, whatever it validated at the load. A LoadModel for
the model it holds, with the options of its load, brings them back (the
llama.cpp backend does both, for a model on context on demand whose
weights can leave the device). Coming back then costs a copy of the
weights, where a model that was stopped costs a load.

LocalAI stops models to make room - the limit of loaded models, a
concurrency group, the memory reclaimer - and until now that was the only
way to give a device back. A model whose file says `park: true` is PARKED
there instead, and brought back by the next request to it.

What decides a park, each time:
- the model's file says `park: true`. Such a model, in a process LocalAI
  is about to stop, is also no longer sent Free on its way to being
  STOPPED: for it Free is not a prelude to a kill - it would copy the
  weights to host memory for a process about to die - and stopping the
  process frees the device, as for every backend that has no Free. (One
  reached by address has no process to stop, and is sent Free as before.);
- the eviction wants the DEVICE back: the limit of loaded models, a
  concurrency group, the memory reclaimer when it watches a GPU. An idle
  timeout wants the model gone, and a reclaimer that watches the host's RAM
  would only fill it further: both stop the model, as before;
- a host memory floor is set (the runtime setting park_host_floor_bytes,
  0 by default: nothing is parked). Parked weights are host memory, and
  the host is not what it was an hour ago: its available memory is read
  before EACH park - the host's, and under a container's memory limit no
  more than the limit still allows - less what the parks in flight are
  about to copy, and the model is parked only if the weights fit above the
  floor at that moment. Otherwise it is stopped, as before;
- the backend says what parking would move (Status, "park_size") and
  answers Free with success and "parked".

A model that was not busy when an eviction selected it is never stopped
for being busy at the moment of the park - a request that arrived, or a
load, a return or a stop of it holding its lifecycle lock, which is tried
and never waited for: the eviction fails, the model is kept, and the
caller selects again. (An eviction selected WHILE busy exists only where
the operator asked for it - force_eviction_when_busy, the busy timeout -
and is the forced stop it was.) Busy is the code FailedPrecondition AND a
message that begins "busy: ": the code alone is also a model the backend
does not hold.

A park that has been sent is followed to its end whatever its caller does.
A backend that answers Free without parking, or has not answered in 60 s,
cannot be trusted with requests: it is stopped, and the caller is told so.
A backend that says the park failed moved nothing: the model stays loaded.

A parked model:
- stays in the list of loaded models - it has a live process - but is not
  one of them for the watchdog: not counted against the limit, not an
  eviction candidate, not idle- or busy-checked, until it comes back.
  LocalAI never stops a parked model by itself; /backend/shutdown does;
- comes back at the next request to it, before the request goes, under
  the model's lifecycle lock: the limits are enforced as for a load (the
  models it may not share the device with are evicted or parked first),
  it is one of the loaded models again from the moment its LoadModel is
  sent - so a load that starts during the copy counts it - and then one
  LoadModel with the options of its load. Of several requests one brings
  it back and the others find it back. /backend/load does the same, for a
  caller that knows what comes next;
- if the device has no room for it then, the request is refused and the
  model stays parked: a deferral, the next request asks again;
- if the return FAILS for any other reason - the backend says so, holds
  the model under other options, is gone, or has not answered in 60 s -
  its process is stopped. The same LoadModel to the same process would
  fail the same way for ever, and nothing else stops a parked model. The
  request that met the failure goes on to an ordinary load, from the file;
- a return that has been sent is followed to its end whatever its caller
  does: a request that leaves during the copy must not leave the weights
  on the device under a record that says "parked";
- is not probed by the failover prober, whose inference would bring its
  weights back behind the loader's back.

For a caller that manages the device itself:
- POST /backend/park {"model"} parks a loaded model. It waits for nothing
  but the park itself and never stops a model that works; when the model
  is not parked the answer says why and the caller decides: 409 model_busy
  (take another); 412 cannot_park / parking_off / host_memory_floor (stop
  it instead, if room is still wanted); 404 model_not_loaded - written by
  the route, so that it differs from the 404 of a server that has no such
  route; 500 model_stopped when the backend broke the park and was
  stopped (the device is free, the model is not loaded);
- GET /system says, for a model that parks, "parks": true and "state":
  "loaded" or "parked", and for a parked one "parked": {"size_host",
  "size_vram_needed", "since"}. size_vram keeps its meaning: what the
  process holds on the device now. A model that does not park is listed
  as it always was.

"The device has no room now" is a 503. A backend's refusal of the moment -
gRPC ResourceExhausted whose message begins "no_room_on_device: " - was a
500 like any fault. It is answered 503 with Retry-After and the type
no_room_on_device by the error handler, by /backend/load, and as an error
event of that type when it comes in the middle of a chat stream. The code
alone is not enough: ResourceExhausted is also a stream that is busy and a
message over the size limit, which stay what they were. The embeddings
route no longer rewrites such a refusal as "conversation too long", and a
load refused for room does not arm the load-failure cooldown: the caller
told to ask again must not find the model locked out. Routes that write
their own error bodies (/v1/messages, /v1/responses) answer 500 as before.

Tests: the loader against a scripted backend (40 specs: what parks, what
does not and why, the lock tried and not waited for, the host read at each
park and two parks not counting the same bytes, a park whose caller
leaves, a Free that does not answer, the return with the options of the
load, a refused return, each way a return fails and the stop that follows,
a return whose caller leaves, a model swapped under a waiting request, the
stop of a model in a real process that sends no Free); the loader and a
real watchdog together (8 specs: a parked model leaves the registry and is
back in it before its weights move, a return makes room as a load does,
two requests and one return, the limit parks, a held lock keeps the model,
the idle timeout stops, the reclaimer parks for a device and stops for
RAM); the watchdog's routing; the real gRPC client's FreeResult; the
container-aware host read; `park: true` from a model's file to its load
options; the runtime setting reaching the loader; the endpoint, /system,
/backend/load's 503, the error body, the stream's event. A model that
does not park: every existing spec of pkg/model, pkg/grpc, pkg/xsysinfo,
core/config, core/backend, core/schema and core/http/endpoints/openai
passes.

